lesson g3-taking-away-in-columns v=1 format=worked grade=3 unit=2 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Taking away in columns"
  goal "Take away three-digit numbers, exchanging across the columns, and use taking away to compare and to work back."

  example {
    worked columns.sub-three-digit a=532 b=278 easy-level=medium
    say """
      Two take eight will not go, so exchange a ten: twelve take eight is four. Now two tens take
      seven will not go either, so exchange a hundred.
      """
  }
  exercises {
    practice columns.sub-three-digit count=2 seed=4
    practice columns.sub-zero count=2 seed=6
    practice sub.farms-compare count=2 seed=3
    practice sub.fair-at-first count=2 hard-count=1 seed=8
    level hard {
      practice sub.robot-mistake count=1 seed=3
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show sub.robot-mistake ah=7 at=6 ao=4 bh=3 bt=2 bo=8 m=0 hard-at=3 hard-bt=5 hard-m=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show sub.biggest-difference a=3 b=8 c=5 easy-a=2 easy-b=4 easy-c=3 hard-a=1 hard-b=9 hard-c=5
  }

  grown-ups """
    Zeros are the hard case. If the child gets stuck on 704, do it with rods and hundreds flats
    once and the exchange becomes obvious. The sheep question compares two amounts, so nothing goes
    away in the story, and the fair question starts with the unknown, so it is solved by adding:
    ask which number is bigger before any working. In the two-star question the child has to say
    which mistake the robot made as well as fix it. In the three-star question, trying two or three
    pairs and noticing which gives the biggest gap is the method.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level only the ones are exchanged in the column sums to do and the sheep comparison, the zero sums have a few ones to start from, the fair numbers add with no carry, the robot's sum is as written, the smaller digit taken from the bigger, and the cards are 2, 3 and 4."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the tens are exchanged as well as the ones, in the column sums and the sheep comparison, the zero sums take away from a whole hundred, the one fair question carries twice, the robot forgot the exchanged ten in a sum that needs two exchanges and is asked twice, and the cards are 1, 5 and 9."
  }
}
